p5_extra = [
    "Mastering native memory lifetime management is the defining skill that separates novice addon developers from seasoned systems architects. In pure JavaScript, developers are accustomed to a garbage-collected world where objects are automatically collected once they become unreachable. In C++, memory must be explicitly managed: every `malloc` must be paired with `free`, and every `new` must be paired with `delete`. When bridging these two worlds, you must establish an ironclad contract between V8's non-deterministic garbage collection and C++'s deterministic destruction.",
    "The fundamental danger arises because V8's garbage collector operates based on heap pressure. V8 tracks the number of bytes allocated in its New Space and Old Space. When a native addon allocates memory via operating system calls, that memory lives outside V8's heap in the process's unmanaged virtual memory address space. To V8, a JavaScript object that wraps a four-gigabyte native machine learning model appears as a tiny JavaScript object weighing less than forty bytes! Because V8 perceives the heap as nearly empty, it will not trigger garbage collection, leaving the massive native model allocated until the entire server process crashes due to system-level Out-Of-Memory (OOM) killer intervention.",
    "`napi_adjust_external_memory` provides the feedback loop that solves this disparity. When your addon allocates native memory, calling `napi_adjust_external_memory(env, bytesAllocated, &adjusted)` directly informs V8 of the external weight. V8 factors this external pressure into its GC scheduling algorithms. If external memory grows rapidly, V8 proactively triggers Scavenger and Mark-Sweep cycles, ensuring that unreferenced wrapper objects are identified and their associated native memory is promptly reclaimed.",
    "Equally important is the `napi_add_finalizer` callback. When wrapping a native C++ object into a JavaScript handle, you register a finalizer function. You must ensure that the finalizer function itself is non-blocking, thread-safe, and free of side effects that attempt to invoke JavaScript code. Because finalizers are executed during or immediately following garbage collection, attempting to invoke arbitrary JavaScript from inside a finalizer can corrupt V8's internal state. By keeping finalizers strictly focused on releasing native resources (closing file descriptors, freeing heap memory, or releasing GPU buffers), you ensure flawless, rock-solid reliability under enterprise production workloads."
]

